Abstract

Modern mass education was designed around a fundamental scalability constraint: one teacher must simultaneously educate many students, despite substantial differences in prior knowledge, learning pace, interests, misconceptions, motivation, language, cognitive strategies, and educational needs. This constraint, inherited from the industrial era, has produced a structural compromise: standardization of instruction at the cost of individualization of learning.

This dissertation proposes a new architectural paradigm for education: the Global Agentic Education Infrastructure (GAEI).

The central proposition is that artificial intelligence — particularly interoperable Large Language Models — can transform the student–teacher relationship by introducing a persistent AI learning agent for each student, while preserving the teacher, the school, and the educational institution as the primary human governance layer.

Under the proposed architecture, each learner interacts with an AI agent through an accessible device. The agent maintains a structured, longitudinal, inspectable representation of the learner's educational state: demonstrated knowledge, competencies, misconceptions, interests, questions, learning history, goals, affect, metacognition, and areas requiring intervention. Teachers receive a pedagogical control interface through which they observe classroom-level and student-level analytics, evaluate student work, diagnose difficulties, intervene when necessary, and orchestrate learning activities. School administrators receive aggregated institutional dashboards rather than unrestricted access to raw individual data.

The architecture does not propose eliminating teachers or abolishing educational institutions. It proposes removing the assumption that every learner must receive the same instruction, at the same time, at the same speed, through the same pedagogical pathway.

The dissertation further proposes that curricula evolve from rigid synchronized sequences toward a shared knowledge foundation combined with personalized exploration. A central mechanism introduced here is Knowledge Seeding: the deliberate exposure of learners to domains, concepts, problems, cultures, scientific phenomena, artistic works, and intellectual questions they may not independently encounter — because curiosity itself is partly dependent on prior exposure.

The resulting educational loop is:

Exposure → Curiosity → Question → Exploration → Learning → Creation → Reflection → New Curiosity.

The dissertation proposes an interoperable, multi-LLM architecture capable of using multiple AI providers rather than depending on a single model, enabling global deployment across languages, cultures, and educational systems.

The work also investigates economic sustainability. A globally deployable educational infrastructure could create substantial demand for AI inference, educational software, institutional subscriptions, infrastructure services, model APIs, and specialized educational agents. However, the dissertation argues that economic value must remain subordinate to educational quality, child protection, privacy, human agency, and institutional accountability.

The ultimate objective is to formulate a technical, pedagogical, economic, and governance framework for a transition from mass standardized education toward globally scalable, individually adaptive, teacher-governed education.

Keywords: agentic education, learner modeling, LLM tutoring, knowledge seeding, teacher orchestration, multi-LLM interoperability, educational governance, privacy by design, adaptive learning, human–AI symbiosis.

