Chapter 2 — State of the Art and Scientific Gap

2.1 Purpose

Before proposing an architecture, we must demonstrate that no existing system already covers it. This chapter surveys eight research and practice domains, then produces a gap matrix.

2.2 Learner Modeling and Knowledge Tracing

Learner modeling has a long history.

· Bayesian Knowledge Tracing (Corbett & Anderson, 1995) models skill mastery probabilistically.
· Deep Knowledge Tracing (Piech et al., 2015) uses recurrent neural networks over interaction sequences.
· Item Response Theory and Knowledge Space Theory (Doignon & Falmagne, 1985) model latent ability and knowledge states.
· Open Learner Models (Bull & Kay, 2007, 2010) expose the model to the learner for reflection and metacognition.
· Learning Analytics (Siemens & Long, 2011) analyzes traces at scale.
· xAPI / Experience API and Calipers provide interoperability standards.

These systems model cognitive state. They rarely model curiosity, goals, or open questions as first-class variables. They are usually tied to a specific platform and do not integrate with a persistent LLM agent.

2.3 Intelligent Tutoring Systems

· ACT-R / Cognitive Tutors (Anderson et al., 1995).
· Cognitive Tutor / MATHia (Carnegie Learning).
· ALEKS (Falmagne et al., 2006).
· AutoTutor (Graesser et al., 2005).
· VanLehn (2011) meta-analysis: ITS produce moderate-to-large effect sizes compared to conventional instruction.

ITS are typically domain-specific, curriculum-bound, and rule-based. They do not use general-purpose LLMs, do not support open-ended curiosity, and rarely include teacher orchestration dashboards at scale.

2.4 Open Learner Models and Metacognition

· Bull & Kay (2010): OLMs support reflection, planning, and self-regulation.
· Mitrovic & Martin (2007): OLMs improve learning outcomes in constraint-based tutors.

OLMs are typically embedded in a single system. They do not speak, do not reason in natural language, and do not orchestrate across subjects.

2.5 Teacher Orchestration and Dashboards

· Dillenbourg & Jermann (2010): orchestration as the real-time management of classroom activities.
· Molenaar & Knoop-van Campen (2017): teacher dashboards in blended learning.
· Verbert et al. (2014): learning analytics dashboards.

Existing dashboards are usually retrospective, aggregated, and not connected to a persistent per-student model or an LLM agent.

2.6 LLM-based Tutoring and Conversational Agents

· Khanmigo (Khan Academy).
· Harvard CS50 bot.
· Duolingo Max.
· Squirrel AI.
· LearnLM (Google).
· Numerous research prototypes (2022–2025).

These systems demonstrate LLM tutoring capability. However, they typically:

· lack a persistent, structured, longitudinal learner state;
· lack teacher orchestration at scale;
· lack multi-LLM interoperability;
· lack governance by design for minors;
· lack knowledge seeding as a pedagogical mechanism.

2.7 Knowledge Seeding and Curiosity

Curiosity research:

· Loewenstein (1994): information gap theory.
· Kidd & Hayden (2015): curiosity as a drive for information.
· Gottlieb et al. (2013): neural basis of curiosity.
· Gruber et al. (2014): curiosity and memory consolidation.

Educational interest research:

· Hidi & Renninger (2006): four-phase model of interest development.
· Krapp (2002): interest and learning.

No existing system implements deliberate, systematic, curriculum-aware knowledge seeding as an architectural mechanism.

2.8 Multi-LLM Interoperability and Educational Standards

· IMS Global / 1EdTech standards (LTI, OneRoster, CASE).
· SCORM, xAPI, Caliper.
· Model Context Protocol (MCP) and emerging agentic standards.

Existing standards do not define learner-state schemas, agent interfaces, or multi-LLM orchestration for education.

2.9 Governance, Privacy, and Child Protection

· GDPR, COPPA, FERPA, UNCRC.
· UNESCO (2021, 2023): guidance on AI in education.
· IEEE 7000-series: ethically aligned design.
· Council of Europe: AI and education reports.

Existing governance frameworks are principles, not architectures. They do not specify how to build an AI educational system that is compliant by construction.

