Chapter 6 — The Persistent Learner State Model

6.1 From Conversation to Structure

A simple LLM tutor remembers conversation. A persistent learner model constructs a structured representation of the learner over time.

6.2 Formal Definition

L_i(t) = [K_i(t), S_i(t), C_i(t), G_i(t), M_i(t), Q_i(t), H_i(t), A_i(t), Met_i(t), P_i(t), E_i(t)]

where:

· K_i(t) — demonstrated knowledge (concepts, facts, procedures);
· S_i(t) — demonstrated competencies and skills;
· C_i(t) — interests and curiosity signals;
· G_i(t) — educational goals;
· M_i(t) — misconceptions and unresolved difficulties;
· Q_i(t) — questions and open problems;
· H_i(t) — longitudinal learning history;
· A_i(t) — affect, motivation, engagement;
· Met_i(t) — metacognitive strategies;
· P_i(t) — interaction preferences and accessibility;
· E_i(t) — ethical context: consent, privacy, constraints.

6.3 Dynamics

L_i(t+1) = f(L_i(t), x_t, a_t, \tau_t)

where:

· x_t — learner–agent interaction;
· a_t — agent action;
· \tau_t — teacher intervention.

6.4 Policies

Agent policy:

\pi_{agent}(a \mid L_i(t), \text{curriculum}, \text{teacher constraints})

Teacher policy:

\pi_{teacher}(\tau \mid \text{dashboard}, L_i(t), \text{needs})

Learner policy:

\pi_{learner}(\text{choice} \mid L_i(t), \text{goals}, \text{curiosity})

6.5 What the Model Must Not Claim

The model must not claim to measure:

· abstract intelligence;
· fixed potential;
· the “worth” of a learner;
· personality;
· character.

The model measures observable, pedagogically meaningful, and revisable variables:

· mastery;
· demonstrated competencies;
· conceptual understanding;
· problem-solving performance;
· progress;
· persistence;
· misconceptions;
· knowledge gaps;
· learning outcomes.

6.6 Open Learner Model

The learner has the right to see, question, and correct their own model. This is both an ethical requirement and a pedagogical mechanism (Bull & Kay, 2010).

6.7 Longitudinality

The model persists across years, not just sessions. This is one of the largest differences between conventional educational software and a persistent AI agent.

6.8 Inspectability and Auditability

Every update to L_i(t) must be:

· logged;
· attributable;
· explainable;
· reversible (if erroneous);
· access-controlled.

